### Tested versions

v4.4.beta2.mono.official [a013481b0]




### System information

win, net9

### Issue description

```
Vector3.Right.SignedAngleTo(new Vector3(-1, 0, 1f), Vector3.Up) == -2.3561945f
Vector3.Right.SignedAngleTo(new Vector3(-1, 1, 1f), Vector3.Up) == -2.186276f
```

```c#
        Vector3 vec = new(0.9878619f, 0.85237634f, -0.66828835f);
        Vector3 axis = new(0.6650185f, -0.51904005f, 0.53698033f);
        var angle = 1.5270251f;
        Assert.That(axis.IsNormalized());
        vec.SignedAngleTo(vec.Rotated(axis, angle), axis)
            .ShouldBe(angle, 0.001);
```

Both fail (and many other cases with fuzzing), and I would expect it to pass (docs imply projection - "when viewed", and it should be coherent with Rotated). 

This acts a bit like a precision issue, but I suspect the underlying issue is more that the implementation isn't working strictly in the axis-normal plane. If this is actually an issue, then I can PR this fix.

```c#
    public static float SignedAngleTo2(this Vector3 thi, Vector3 to, Vector3 axis)
    {
        var vY = axis.Cross(thi);
        var vX = vY.Cross(axis);
        return Mathf.Atan2(to.Dot(vY), to.Dot(vX));
    }
```

This seems to have better accuracy (~e-05) with a bunch of random testing against Rotated, (surprisingly without normalization of the inputs). It does cost an extra cross product though, but I think its necessary to realign the input vector with the axis-plane.


### Steps to reproduce

Asserts above

